{"qid":"Q1322662","names":["Principality of Chernigov"],"title":"Principality of Chernigov","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Principality of Chernigov was one of the major Rus' principalities, centered on the city of Chernigov on the Desna River and controlling substantial territory in left-bank Ukraine and the upper Oka basin. At its apex in the late 11th and early 12th centuries under princes of the Sviatoslavichi line, it rivaled Kyiv in prestige and commanded the prosperous Tmutarakan enclave on the Black Sea. Internecine struggle among Rus' princes steadily fragmented its territories, and the Mongol invasion of 1237–1241 devastated the principality: Chernigov was sacked in 1239, and Prince Mykhailo of Chernigov was executed by Batu Khan's court in 1246 for refusing to perform obeisance. The principality never recovered as an independent political unit.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The principality's core territory is reasonably well attested through chronicle sources, but its outer boundaries fluctuated with dynastic politics and are imprecisely mapped for most of the period.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Chernigov Principality","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Chernigov-principality"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Principality of Chernigov","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principality_of_Chernigov"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Principality of Chernigov","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Mstislav of Chernigov","nativeName":"Мстислав Володимирович","roman":"Mstyslav Volodymyrovych","start":1024,"end":1036,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":n